    "bioEn": "Kamsiyochi Onyekere is a recent Honours Bachelor of Science graduate from the University of Toronto Mississauga, specializing in Biology for Health Science, Psychology, and Biomedical Communications. She is deeply passionate about expanding access to STEM for Black and underrepresented students, a commitment she has turned into action as the Founder and President of Black in STEM, where she built workshops, mentorship opportunities, and academic support programming aimed at breaking down the systemic barriers so many students face in academic and professional spaces. Through these initiatives, she has partnered with student organizations, faculty, and community groups to create outreach and academic support that centers equity and representation. She is equally passionate about mental health in Black communities, driven to see mental health spaces and resources become more accessible, culturally responsive, and reflective of the people they serve. Her research experience at St. Michael's Hospital, supporting studies on intimate partner violence, brain injury, and community health equity, reflects this same commitment to community-centred work that improves outcomes for underserved populations.",
